WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:08.849
Do you remember the first computer you
ever used? Or the TV you once watched

00:00:08.849 --> 00:00:12.929
cartoons on? it's easy to take modern
technology for granted, but if you

00:00:12.929 --> 00:00:16.800
compare it to the room-size computers of
the 1960's, you'll notice how quickly

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.600
things have changed! Or rather, how much
they've shrunk! As technology advances,

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.890
the amount of data we need to store and
manipulate is increasing, and the size of

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:30.210
devices is decreasing so much that
conventional techniques of data storage

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:35.579
won't be good enough for long. Most
modern technology is electronic: it uses

00:00:35.579 --> 00:00:38.760
a property of electrons called charge to
encode information.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:43.680
One example is computer memory, often
called RAM, which is where computers keep

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:47.969
information they're working with for
several seconds or minutes. RAM contains

00:00:47.969 --> 00:00:53.640
billions of tiny plates, called
capacitors, that hold electrons. To store

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:57.780
information, computers send a current of
electrons into the capacitors; some of

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:02.219
these electrons collect on the plates. If
the number of electrons on a capacitor

00:01:02.219 --> 00:01:07.260
is above a threshold, the computer
assigns it a value of one; otherwise, it's a

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:12.180
zero. Unique patterns of zeros and ones
encode information like letters, numbers

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:17.100
and images. As chips get smaller, so do
capacitors, and we are faced with the

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:21.420
problem of charge leakage--electrons
constantly slip away from the plates.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:26.909
When the electron count falls below the
threshold, the stored data is lost. To

00:01:26.909 --> 00:01:30.479
avoid this, computers have to recharge
their capacitors several times per

00:01:30.479 --> 00:01:35.810
second, which requires more and more
power as chips shrink.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:39.810
Fortunately, an emerging technology
called "spintronics" could solve this

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:45.240
problem. Spintronics uses spin, another
property of electrons, to encode data. The

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:50.700
spin of an electron is either "up" or "down." It is helpful to think of "spin up"

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:55.140
electrons as little magnets with their
north poles pointing up, and "spin down"

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:59.880
electrons with downwards pointing north
poles. Data is stored based on the spin

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:03.899
states of electrons that are already
present. This means that there's no

00:02:03.899 --> 00:02:08.340
problem of "leakage" with spintronics- once information is stored, it stays there

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:13.200
until we want to change it! The spin
-states of electrons change if we

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.519
actively influence them by exposing them
to magnetic fields. To take advantage of

00:02:17.519 --> 00:02:22.530
this, scientists developed magneto
resistive RAM, which replaces capacitors

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:28.079
with tunneling magnetoresistance
structures. TMR structures are made up of

00:02:28.079 --> 00:02:33.569
an "insulator" sandwiched between two
"ferromagnetic" materials. An insulator is

00:02:33.569 --> 00:02:37.920
a material with very high resistivity-
this means that electrons are very

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:39.790
unlikely to move through it.

00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:42.720
A "ferromagnet" is a material in which the spins

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:48.480
tend to align along the same direction. The lower ferromagnetic layer is "fixed"--

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:53.160
its electrons are forced to have the
same spin as each other by an adjacent

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:58.709
strong magnet. The upper ferromagnetic
layer is "free"-- its electrons change their

00:02:58.709 --> 00:03:03.000
spins in response to external magnetic
fields, or through a phenomenon called

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.739
spin-transfer torque, which is where
electrons change their spins to match

00:03:07.739 --> 00:03:12.569
the spins of nearby flowing electrons. When the spins of the ferromagnetic

00:03:12.569 --> 00:03:20.250
layers are parallel, the structure stores
a 1. Opposite spins represent a 0. To read

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:24.870
the data and determine whether it's a 0
or a 1, the computer sends a tiny current

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:29.130
through the TMR structure. If the spins
of the top and bottom layers are

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:34.049
parallel, electrons can "tunnel" through
the insulator. "Tunneling" is a process

00:03:34.049 --> 00:03:38.130
that is similar to a basketball moving
through a solid wall- it's normally very

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:41.730
unlikely, but parallel spins make it
possible.

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:46.709
If the spins are opposite, almost no
electrons tunnel through. By seeing how

00:03:46.709 --> 00:03:50.760
much current reaches the other side ,  a computer can tell whether the structure is

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:57.230
holding a 1 or 0. To write data, we send
larger currents up or down the structure.

00:03:57.230 --> 00:04:01.410
Sending electrons up the structure, from
the fixed layer to the free layer,

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:06.900
switches the value from a 0 to 1. Because
of spin transferred torque, the flowing

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:11.430
electrons adopt the fixed layer's spin as
they move through it. The electrons in

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:16.600
the free layer then flipped to match the
spins of the flowing electrons.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:21.400
Since the free layer responds to changes
in external magnetic fields--which are

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:26.290
generated by both computers and living
organisms--TMR structures have numerous

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:30.910
applications beyond computers. When a
neuron in your brain fires, your

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:36.310
heartbeats, or a white blood cell attacks
a virus, tiny magnetic fields are created.

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:41.050
A spintronic sensor chip could detect
the smallest deviations from your "normal"

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:45.330
magnetic fields detecting anything from
bacterial infections to brain tumors.

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:50.020
Because magnetic fields are not altered
by the structures they move through, it

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:54.670
could even do this from outside the body!
And because electron spin changes our

00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:59.410
near-instant spintronics technology
could do so quickly . Ultimately,

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:04.450
spintronics technology holds promise in
countless areas, from computer memory to

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:10.880
medicine and beyond!

